# Two modules declaring the same PUBLIC top-level name.
#   zerg build examples/1g/pubname/main.zg
# Expected output:
#   2
#   3
#
# VISIBILITY IS THE FILE'S AND A PUBLIC NAME IS NOT PROGRAM-GLOBAL (#92). Both modules declare
# `helper`, and the specification says they are two different names — `docs/runtime/package.md`
# declines a global registry on purpose, so there is nothing for a public name to be unique
# WITHIN. What used to collide was the C symbol, and only that: a PRIVATE name two modules both
# declare has been told apart by a module tag all along, and the tag was simply not given to a
# public one.
#
# The qualified call is what chooses. `a.helper` and `b.helper` name the module out loud, so the
# row read for visibility and the symbol emitted are the NAMESPACE's — not the caller's, which
# is what every call site asked for before and is right only for a private name.
import (
	"./one"
	"./two"
)

fn main() {
	print one.helper(1)
	print two.helper(1)
}
